WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:03.780
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:10.740
In a previous episode not so long ago, we
burned down a virtual tree.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:16.800
This was possible through an amazing simulation
paper from 4 years ago, where each leaf has

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:23.940
its own individual mass and area, they burn
individually, transfer heat to their surroundings,

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:31.710
and finally, branches bend, and, look can
eventually even break in this process.

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:33.850
How quickly did this run?

00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:35.479
Of course, in real time.

00:00:35.479 --> 00:00:41.989
Well, that is quite a paper, so if this was
so good, how does anyone improve that?

00:00:41.989 --> 00:00:44.609
Burn down another virtual tree?

00:00:44.609 --> 00:00:48.179
No, no, that would be too easy.

00:00:48.179 --> 00:00:53.539
You know what, instead, let’s set on fire
an entire virtual forest.

00:00:53.539 --> 00:00:54.629
Oh yeah!

00:00:54.629 --> 00:01:00.539
Here you see a simulation of a devastating
fire from a lightning strike in Yosemite national

00:01:00.539 --> 00:01:01.600
park.

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:06.770
The simulations this time around are typically
sped up a great deal to be able to give us

00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:12.039
a better view of how it spreads, so if you
see some flickering, that is the reason for

00:01:12.039 --> 00:01:13.039
that.

00:01:13.039 --> 00:01:16.990
But wait, is that really that much harder?

00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:22.310
Why not just put a bunch of trees next to
each other and start the simulation?

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:23.659
Would that work?

00:01:23.659 --> 00:01:25.760
The answer is a resounding no.

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:31.030
Let’s have a look why, and with that, hold
on to your papers because here comes the best

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:37.499
part: it also simulates not only the fire,
but cloud dynamics as well.

00:01:37.499 --> 00:01:43.960
Here you see how the wildfire creates lots
of hot, and dark smoke closer to the ground,

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:48.530
and, wait for it…yes!

00:01:48.530 --> 00:01:49.530
There we go!

00:01:49.530 --> 00:01:54.579
Higher up, the condensation of water creates
this lighter, cloudy region.

00:01:54.579 --> 00:02:00.180
Yes, this is key to the simulation, not just
because of the aesthetic effects, but this

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:05.689
wildfire can indeed create a cloud type that
goes by the name flammagenitus.

00:02:05.689 --> 00:02:09.310
So, is that good or bad news?

00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:10.920
Well, both!

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:16.370
Let’s start with the good news: it often
produces rainfall, which helps putting out

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:17.470
the fire.

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:22.260
Well, that is wonderful news, so then, what
is so bad about it?

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:29.030
Well, flammagenitus clouds may also trigger
a thunderstorm, and thus, create another huge

00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:30.030
fire.

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:32.220
That’s bad news number one.

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:38.120
And bad news number two: it also occludes
the fire, thereby making it harder to locate

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:39.689
and extinguish it.

00:02:39.689 --> 00:02:47.010
So, got it, add cloud dynamics to the tree
fire simulator, and we are done, right?

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:49.159
No, not even close.

00:02:49.159 --> 00:02:53.210
In a forest fire simulation, not just clouds,
everything matters.

00:02:53.210 --> 00:02:59.079
For instance, first we need to take into consideration
the wind intensity and direction.

00:02:59.079 --> 00:03:05.670
This can mean the difference between a manageable
or a devastating forest fire.

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:12.819
Second, it takes into consideration the density
and moisture intensity of different tree types

00:03:12.819 --> 00:03:19.420
- for instance, you see that the darker trees
here are burning down really slowly.

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:20.420
Why is that?

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:26.099
This is because these trees are denser birches
and oak trees.

00:03:26.099 --> 00:03:30.400
Third, the distribution of the trees also
matter.

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:34.900
Of course, the more the area is covered by
trees, the more degrees of freedom there are

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:37.409
for the fire to spread.

00:03:37.409 --> 00:03:40.510
And, fourth.

00:03:40.510 --> 00:03:45.790
Fire can not only spread horizontally from
tree to tree, but vertically too.

00:03:45.790 --> 00:03:46.790
Look!

00:03:46.790 --> 00:03:50.640
When a small tree catches fire, this can happen.

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:56.900
So, as we established from the previous paper,
one tree catching fire can be simulated in

00:03:56.900 --> 00:03:58.660
real time.

00:03:58.660 --> 00:04:00.560
What about an entire forest?

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:06.590
Let’s take the simulation with the most
number of trees, my goodness, they simulated

00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:09.879
120k trees there.

00:04:09.879 --> 00:04:13.680
And the computation time for one simulation
step was…95.

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:17.660
So, 95 what?

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:20.310
95 milliseconds.

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:21.630
Wow!

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:27.080
So this thing runs interactively, which means
that all of these phenomena can be simulated

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:28.730
in close to real time.

00:04:28.730 --> 00:04:35.440
With that, we can now model how a fire would
spread in real forests around the world, test

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:41.160
different kinds of fire barriers and their
advantages, and we can even simulate how to

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:44.030
effectively put out the fire.

00:04:44.030 --> 00:04:50.140
And don’t forget, we went from simulating
one burning tree to a hundred and twenty thousand

00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:52.930
in just one more paper down the line.

00:04:52.930 --> 00:05:06.950
What a time to 

00:05:06.950 --> 00:05:28.210
be alive!

00:05:28.210 --> 00:05:38.139
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

